 It came during his current term, and well over 10% voted No, so the near-unanimous rule doesn't apply.\n- **The provision:** Section 220.5(a) of the bill text forbids any school policy that \"would require an employee or a contractor to disclose\" a pupil's gender identity \"without the pupil's consent.\" That matches the rung's wording directly.\n- **Why not rung 1:** Section 220.3 only says staff \"shall not be required to disclose.\" It doesn't require them to keep the information from parents, so it falls short of \"Require staff to keep a student's gender identity from parents.\"\n- **Shape:** Both passages are coded `chair-shaped` and listed in `rests_on`. They are one record, so code will tier this chair `single-source`.\n\nTwo calls a reviewer might dispute:\n- **Preemption (Q10):** the bill stops schools and districts from requiring disclosure, which normally makes a passage `adjacent`. I coded it `on-question` because rung 2 is itself about forbidding those rules.\n- **Single subject:** I treated the bill as one subject. Its other parts (parent resources and protection for staff from retaliation) are on the same matter, so I did not code it `multi-subject`.\n\nI left `needs_source` empty and added no candidate quotes.","ttft_ms":10479,"type":"result","duration_ms":28347,"uuid":"83abfd94-d1cf-4831-8c33-300a0368279f","ttft_stream_ms":2083,"time_to_request_ms":276,"first_content_frame_ms":2088,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
